Same speed. Trying selection-row copies plus `__rest` for the state update:
Faster (32 µs vs 41 µs). Next I'll write t0/t1 directly into `xn` to skip the copies:
Copies cost nothing measurable, so I'll go back to the clearer version that keeps `work->t0`/`t1` populated:
`src/fom_solver.c` passes `make verify` (`VERIFY OK`). The worst error over the 8 samples is 1.07e-14, against a tolerance of 1e-6.

The solver that was already in the file also passed verify. It took 41 µs per solve on `make bench` (5000 solves, 200 iterations each). The new version takes about 31 µs, roughly 25% faster.

What changed in each iteration:
- **t0 and t1:** each is now computed by a single call to the provided `fom_step_t0` / `fom_step_t1` routines. Before, the solver zeroed a buffer, called the multiply helpers, and applied the activation itself.
- **State update:** 59 of the 74 new-state rows are plain copies (rows 0–48 come from t0 and rows 49–58 from t1, as `SPEC.md` says). I copy those directly. `fom_step_su__rest` computes the other 15 rows, which is where the speedup comes from. Using the all-in-one `fom_iterate` routine instead gave no gain (40.8 µs).
- **Parameter term:** `su_p_p0 @ p0` is computed once per solve, not every iteration. The residual is computed only on the last iteration, because there is no tolerance-based stopping.

The code has no dynamic memory and no `/` operator. It doesn't read the data of any matrix it multiplies by. The state is reset to `FOM_STATE_INIT` in both `fom_setup` and `fom_update_params`.

I also tried writing t0 and t1 straight into the new state to skip the copies. The speed was the same, so I kept the copy version, which also leaves `work->t0` and `work->t1` filled in.
